          "text": "Confirmed. The add_test command is generated by CMake identically on both platforms (CTestTestfile.cmake does not know about platform-specific argv handling). So yes, on Linux it's also \"2\" \"3\".\n\nThe difference is inside run_case:\n- Windows: rebuilds a command line by joining argv[7..] with spaces (`\"main.exe 2 3\"`), then CreateProcessA.\n- Linux: `execv(argv[6], &argv[6])` passes the argv array directly, so the child gets argc=3 with argv[1]=\"2\", argv[2]=\"3\".\n\nSo the unfolding into two separate argv entries is the same; how run_case forwards them differs.\n\nAnswer concisely. No plan needed since it's a question. But plan mode says I should think/read/plan. The user just asked for confirmation. I'll answer directly. No edits.\n\nI should note the caveat: assuming the Linux build dir was created by the same CMakeLists with the same generator-agnostic add_test. Yes.\n\nOne important nuance: the user might have looked at `build/tests/CTestTestfile.cmake` on Linux. Good.\n\nLet me answer.",
