          "text": "The user asks: is this a working protection of spaces inside arguments? Can it be made more idiomatic via some C++ construct?\n\nThe snippet:\n```cpp\nfor (int i = 7; i < argc; ++i) {\n    cmdline += ' ';\n    cmdline += \"\\\"\";\n    cmdline += argv[i];\n    cmdline += \"\\\"\";\n}\n```\n\nTwo questions:\n1. Is it a working protection of spaces inside args?\n2. Can it be made more idiomatic?\n\nAnswer 1: It does protect spaces (each arg is wrapped in quotes, so Windows CRT parsing keeps it as one token). But \"working\" has caveats — Windows command-line quoting is not as simple as wrapping in quotes. The correct algorithm for quoting argv on Windows is complex. Key caveats:\n- An argument that contains a `\"` (double quote) must escape it, and backslashes before a quote need doubling. Wrapping naively breaks for args containing `\"` or trailing backslashes.\n- An empty argument: `\"\"` → CRT yields empty string, that's fine actually.\n- An argument containing `&`, `|`, `^`, `<`, `>` is not a problem with CreateProcess + CRT, because cmd.exe is not involved (CreateProcessA directly). But if the child spawns via cmd, then meta-characters matter. Here CreateProcessA is used directly, so cmd isn't interpreting. Good.\n- Also `\\\"` handling: an argument ending with backslash before closing quote → backslash escapes the quote. E.g. arg `C:\\path\\` → `\"C:\\path\\\"` → the `\\\"` becomes a literal quote and the string isn't closed → breaks. That's the classic bug.\nSo: for the arguments in this project (numbers like 2, 3, +10, 4ch, ++10), it works. But as a general \"protection\", it's not fully correct — it's the naive approach. The fully correct one is the known \"argv quoting\" algorithm (used by MSVC CRT / CommandLineToArgvW inverse), e.g., python's list2cmdline or .NET's.\n\nActually, is there a standard library facility? In C++ there is no std:: facility to build a Windows command line. So no direct STL replacement. On Windows, historically you'd use `CommandLineToArgvW` for the inverse (parse), and there's no inverse function to build. So hand-rolling is the norm. Boost.Process handles it internally (but boost not in this project's stack yet).\n\nNow idiom: can we make it more idiomatic C++?\n\nOptions:\n- Since we need to produce a string, one can build via `std::string` and append. But cleaner: avoid manual index loop; use a range-based approach? We need index i from 7. Could use `std::span` over argv? argv is `char*[]`. Could do:\n  ```cpp\n  std::string cmdline = std::format(\"\\\"{}\\\"\", argv[6]);\n  for (char* arg : std::span(argv + 7, argv + argc)) {\n      cmdline += std::format(\" \\\"{}\\\"\", arg);\n  }\n  ```\n  `std::span` constructor `span(It first, It last)` from pointer iterators is C++20. Actually `std::span(argv + 7, argv + argc)` — span has a (first, last) constructor for contiguous iterators. Hmm, argv is `char**`; `argv + 7` and `argv + argc` are pointers — span<const char* const>? There's `std::span<T>(It first, It last)` requiring sized_sentinel. Pointers work. So `std::span<char*>(argv + 7, argv + argc)` — but argv decays to `char**`. `std::span<char*>(argv + 7, argc - 7)` also works. Hmm, but element type is `char*`, and span<const char* const>? Let's not overcomplicate.\n\n- Idiomatic could use `std::format`/`std::print` to compose. But building cmdline with std::format is fine: `std::format(\"\\\"{}\\\"\", ...)`.\n- Using `std::string::append` vs `+=` — fine.\n- The `'\"'` char literal instead of `\"\\\"\"` string literal — more idiomatic/clarity: `cmdline += '\"';` Note: `cmdline += '\"'` appends a single char; `cmdline += \"\\\"\"` appends a C-string. Both work.\n\nBut the user asks: is it working protection AND can it be more idiomatic. Since plan mode, I should NOT edit. I can propose a plan / show code in the answer as part of planning. But must not write to files.\n\nActually, the user is essentially asking a design question. I should answer and maybe propose options, ask their preference. Since plan mode, I should construct a plan. But the user hasn't explicitly asked to implement — they asked \"может можно её сделать более идиоматичной\". So I should answer, discuss tradeoffs, and propose a plan (not execute).\n\nAlso important critical point: the bigger question — should we quote at all? The user earlier established that quoting changes the test semantics (`t4_..._too_much` would become one arg `2 3` instead of two). So \"защита пробелов\" in run_case would actually break the intended test! Wait — no. Let's think.\n\nCurrently `add_test` passes `\"2\" \"3\"` as two separate CTest arguments → run_case gets argv[7]=\"2\", argv[8]=\"3\". Then run_case builds cmdline `\"exe\" 2 3` → child gets two args. Good.\n\nIf we add quoting in run_case: cmdline `\"exe\" \"2\" \"3\"` → child still gets two args \"2\" and \"3\" (quoting each element separately doesn't merge them). So quoting each argv element individually does NOT change the arg count — it only protects spaces WITHIN an element. So it does not break `t4_..._too_much`. \n\nBut if someone had `RAD_ERR \"2 3\"` (one element), then without quoting run_case builds `\"exe\" 2 3` → child gets two args (Windows re-splits); with quoting → `\"exe\" \"2 3\"` → child gets one arg. That's the difference. So quoting makes Windows consistent with Linux (Linux already passes one arg). That's actually a desirable consistency fix.\n\nSo the user's intent: protect spaces so Windows behaves like Linux and round-trips args faithfully. That's legit and arguably correct.\n\nBut caution about the naive quoting algorithm's correctness with embedded quotes/backslashes. For this project's args (numbers, letters), it's fine. But to be robust, the proper algorithm.\n\nAlso critical: which is the correct approach at all — using a Windows-specific argv→cmdline serialization is fundamentally a workaround, because CreateProcess takes a string. The \"right\" fix is the standard algorithm (or use a library that