it `std::size_t`/`int` fine.\n\nBut is the index loop really a problem? The AGENTS says prefer ranges/iterators. A range-based for reduces index bookkeeping; less chance of off-by-one. Might be a reasonable idiomatic improvement.\n\nHowever, the bigger idiom question: building a command line is inherently string munging; std::format helps readability.\n\nAlso, is there an existing helper in the codebase? No.\n\nNow, should the quoting helper be a function? Only used once → AGENTS § 4.2 says don't create a module/wrapper without added value. A local lambda or just inline loop is fine. But if we want to handle the escaping algorithm correctly, a helper function with a comment would be justified.\n\nNow about correctness of naive quoting: Let me detail the Windows rules (from MS docs \"Parsing C++ command-line arguments\" / CommandLineToArgvW):\n- Whitespace (space/tab) separates args unless inside quotes.\n- A string surrounded by double quotes is one arg.\n- `\\\"` (backslash-quote) → literal quote, and the backslash count rules: 2n backslashes + `\"` → n backslashes and toggle quote mode; 2n+1 backslashes + `\"` → n backslashes + literal quote.\n- Empty string → `\"\"`.\n\nSo naive wrapping breaks for args that contain `\"` or end with `\\`. For this project's arg set, fine. But I should flag it and recommend either documenting the limitation or implementing the standard algorithm.\n\nAlso: there's another subtlety — the program name argv[6] is also quoted (line 185) with the same naive approach; path could contain backslash but not trailing backslash before quote normally (exe path ends with `.exe`). Fine.\n\nAlso note: on the receiving side, it's the CRT that parses. `CreateProcessA` + child CRT. But if the child is a CRT-initialized C/C++ program (all of ours are), yes. Consistent.\n\nNow, is there an even more idiomatic/robust option? Use `std::filesystem::path` for the exe? Not needed.\n\nAnother thought: Instead of building a cmdline manually at all, is there a Windows API to launch with an argv array directly? No — CreateProcessW only takes a string. (There's `execv` emulation via `_spawnv` in the CRT! `_spawnv`, `_spawnvp` from `<process.h>` on Windows — these take an argv array and handle quoting internally.) That's actually a strong alternative: MSVC CRT provides `_spawnv(_P_WAIT, path, argv)` (and `_spawnve`), which take an argv array, do the quoting, and wait. That would remove the whole manual cmdline construction. But it doesn't allow custom std handles redirection (STARTUPINFO) — `_spawnv` has no way to set hStdInput/Output/Error. That's why the code uses CreateProcess with STARTUPINFO. So `_spawnv` doesn't fit. Good to mention why not.\n\nHmm, but is that true? There's `_spawnve` etc. and no handle redirection. Correct, spawn family inherits handles; to redirect you'd have to change the parent's std handles (dup2-like via _dup2) which is messy. So CreateProcess is right. But the manual cmdline is the cost.\n\nSo my answer:\n1. Does it protect spaces? Yes, for args without embedded quotes/trailing backslashes. It's the naive quoting; Windows quoting rules are more complex. For this project's arg values it's safe.\n2. IMPORTANT: quoting each element separately doesn't change the arg count (so `t4_..._too_much` stays two args). It only makes Windows round-trip args that contain spaces the same as Linux. That's a consistency win. But it's still a Windows-only serialization asymmetry.\n3. Idiomatic improvements: use range-based for over a subrange (`std::views::counted` or `std::span`), constant for first-arg index, `std::format` to compose instead of `+=` chains, char literal `'\"'`.\n\nThen propose a plan with options and ask preference. Also flag that the escaping algorithm is the real correctness question, and ask whether they want the full algorithm or documented naive.\n\nSince we're in plan mode, I should present a plan, and ask whether to proceed, and which option. Use the question tool? Could be appropriate