          "text": "Good. So `EMPTY_ARG` is `\"\"` (one empty element) and is passed to t1/t2/t3 which take no args. Let me check what CTestTestfile does with an empty arg: `${args}` unquoted with value being an empty single element. In CMake, an empty string element... unquoted expansion of an empty list element yields nothing? Actually `set(EMPTY_ARG \"\")` sets variable to an empty string (one element which is empty). When expanded unquoted in add_test, it may expand to nothing or to one empty argument. CMake behavior: unquoted `${EMPTY_ARG}` where value is \"\" → expands to nothing (no argument). Actually CMake's list expansion of an empty value yields zero arguments. Hmm. Let me check the generated CTestTestfile for t1_c_empty_code — earlier line 7: `... \"--\" \"...main.exe\")` — no trailing empty arg. So the empty arg vanished entirely. So for t1-t3, no args are passed. Good.\n\nNow, importantly: this means the current pipeline drops empty arguments (on Windows too). With quoting, an empty arg would become `\"\"` and be preserved. But for t1-t3 that's intended (no args). Not relevant.\n\nNow let me finalize the answer. Key points:\n\n1. Functionally yes, it protects spaces for the current values.\n2. Caveat: naive quoting — Windows rules are more complex (embedded `\"`, trailing backslashes). For this project's args (numbers, symbols), fine; but as general \"protection\" not fully correct. Could document the limitation or implement the standard algorithm.\n3. Note: quoting each element separately doesn't change arg count, so no test semantics change; it makes Windows behave like Linux for space-containing args (round-trip fidelity). Arg values currently have no spaces, so behavior unchanged in practice — safe.\n4. Bonus: quoting also preserves empty args (`\"\"`), which currently vanish.\n\nIdiomatic improvements:\n- Replace index loop with range-based for over subrange (`std::views::counted(argv + 7, argc - 7)`), removing the manual index and off-by-one risk (aligns with AGENTS § 4.3 ranges preference).\n- Use `std::format` to compose one line instead of 4 `+=`.\n- Name the index 7 (magic number; § 4.9 magic numbers).\n- `'\"'` char instead of `\"\\\"\"` if not using format.\n\nProposed plan options:\nA) Minimal idiom clean-up only (keep naive quoting).\nB) A + robust quoting helper (standard Windows escaping, with unit test?) \nC) Leave as-is.\n\nAlso mention: line 185 has the same naive quoting for the path.\n\nAlso there's a design question: is hand-rolling cmdline the right approach vs alternatives? Windows has no argv-based CreateProcess; `_spawnv` from CRT takes argv but can't set custom std handles (no STARTUPINFO), so it can't replace this. Worth one line.\n\nNow, plan mode — I must not edit. I'll present analysis and a proposed plan, and ask.\n\nShould I use the question tool? The instructions in plan mode encourage asking. Let me ask one question with options. But the user also may just want info. Hmm. The user asked \"может можно её сделать более идиоматичной через некую с++ конструкцию\" — they're asking for suggestions. I think providing the analysis + suggested construct + asking whether to proceed/which variant is right.\n\nLet me keep it reasonably concise. Provide the code sketch inside the answer (not applied).\n\nLet me write.\n\nActually, on `std::views::counted` — element type from `char**` is `char*`. Loop `for (const char* arg : std::views::counted(argv + 7, argc - 7))`. The count param type is `iter_difference_t<I>` = ptrdiff_t; passing int converts fine. Good.\n\nOr `std::span<char*>(argv + 7, argc - 7)` — span's (first, last) or (ptr, count) constructor; count is size_type (size_t); int converts with maybe sign warning under /W4! `std::span<char*>(argv + 7, static_cast<std::size_t>(argc - 7))`. Hmm /W4 warns C4245? converting int to size_t maybe. To be safe, cast.\n\n`std::views::counted` takes difference_type (signed) so int fits better, no conversion warning. Good choice.\n\nLet me double che