utput-log <file>` to redirect the log. And `--test-dir`.\n- There's also `--output-junit` (CMake 3.21+) to produce JUnit XML.\n- To show passed test output too: `--output-on-failure` only failures; `-V`/`--verbose` shows full output for all tests; `-VV` extra verbose.\n- The exact log path: `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/Testing/Temporary/LastTest.log`. And the timestamped copy in `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/Testing/<YYYYMMDD-HHMM>/Test.xml`? Actually that's for dashboard; local runs may not create it unless `ctest -D`? We saw only Temporary. Right, local `ctest` writes `Testing/Temporary/LastTest.log`, `LastTestsFailed.log`, `CTestCostData.txt`, and `LastTest.log.tmp*`. The date-stamped `Testing/<date>/` is created by CDash submissions or `ctest -D Experimental*`. Actually I think `ctest` also writes `Testing/Temporary/LastTest.log`; the tag/date dirs appear with `-D ExperimentalTest` etc.\n\nIs it worth printing the path? Arguments:\n- Standard practice: tools don't print their log path by default; users know or docs say. But some tools do print \"full log: ...\". CTest itself doesn't. Adding a path line could help discoverability, but it adds noise on every run.\n- Better: document in README how to get full output (`--output-on-failure` / `-V`) and where the log is. And/or use `--output-log`.\n- If they want it in the console automatically, they can run `ctest --output-on-failure` and, on failure, ctest already prints failing test output. So the log path is mostly for \"all tests' output\".\n- Also `CTEST_OUTPUT_ON_FAILURE=1` env var exists.\n\nNow, \"как это обычно делается при прогоне без --output-on-failure\": Without `--output-on-failure`, ctest prints only the progress lines (`1/233 Test #... Passed/Failed`) and a summary, and the \"The following tests FAILED:\" list — but NOT the failing test's stderr/stdout. The details are only in `Testing/Temporary/LastTest.log`. So a common workflow: run `ctest` (quiet), then on failure open LastTest.log or rerun with `--output-on-failure`, or use `--rerun-failed --output-on-failure`.\n\nIs printing the path worth it? My recommendation: \n- Don't add custom printing into test programs; that's not their job. CTest manages the log.\n- If you want discoverability, document it in README (the artifact location is part of the build layout and is `.gitignore`d), and/or provide a convenience: `ctest --test-dir build/debug --output-on-failure` as the documented test command (§4.9 п.1 \"one command\").\n- Optionally, on failure you could print at the end via a custom target/post-test step — but CTest doesn't have a hook to print at the end unless you use `--output-log` and wrap. Not worth it.\n\nAlso there's the aspect: `--output-on-failure` vs `-V`. And `--output-log` lets you set the log location; e.g., `ctest ... --output-log build/debug/last_test.log`. Hmm, but default is fine.\n\nAlso: the log is in the build dir which is gitignored; when they diagnose, path is `build/debug/Testing/Temporary/LastTest.log`. Also `LastTestsFailed.log` (list) — which we discussed.\n\nLet me also mention: there's `ctest --test-dir ... --output-on-failure` and if they want to see passing output too, `-V`.\n\nNow, the user's phrasing: \"есть ли смысл при --output-on-failure выводить путь до LastTestLog ... как это обычно делается при прогоне без --output-on-failure\". So they ask two things:\n(a) Is it worth printing the log path when using --output-on-failure?\n(b) How is it usually done when running without --output-on-failure?\n\nAnswers:\n(a) Generally not necessary; CTest doesn't do it. With --output-on-failure you already see failing output, so the log path adds little. It might help for the \"all tests\" log. If desired, do it via docs or a thin wrapper, not in test programs.\n(b) Without it, ctest prints only Passed/Failed lines + \"The following tests FAILED\" list; the detailed output goes only to Testing/Temporary/LastTest.log. Standard practice: \n   - On failure, rerun `--rerun-failed --output-on-failure`, or \n   - `-V` for full verbose output, or \n   - open the log / grep it.\n   CTest also writes `LastTestsFailed.log` (list of failed test numbers/names). And CTest has `--output-log <path>` to control the log file location, and `--output-junit`.\n\nI could also verify the exact ctest options from `ctest --help` locally (read-only). Let me run `ctest --help` to cite options accurately. That's read-only. But it's a long output; I can grep. Let me do it: `ctest --help` and search for output-log, output-on-failure, output-junit.\n\nLet me run it and filter. Using PowerShell: `ctest --help | Select-String \"output\"`. But instruction says avoid Select-String; use Grep tool? Grep tool works on files, not command output. Hmm. I can run `ctest --help` and read the output. It could be large but fine. Or use `ctest --help-full`? Let me just run `ctest --help` and capture.\n\nActually the bash tool says avoid Select-String. But filtering command output may be acceptable... The rule says prefer dedicated tools for file content. For command output filtering, it's fine-ish. But to be safe, run `ctest --help` fully; it's ~200 lines.\n\nLet me run `ctest --help` (and maybe `cmake --version`). This is read-only.\n\nLet me do it to get accurate option names/descriptions.\n\nThen answer with the plan/recommendation. Since plan mode, no edits; present recommendation and maybe ask if they want a README note or a wrapper.\n\nLet me run ctest --help.",
