          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks two things:\n1. Should they make check.cpp more substantive/precise, or is it already normal per §4.6?\n2. They decided to normalize CRLF in both branches of check (contains AND equal), because for `--contains` the criterion is quite strict, e.g., trailing `\\n`: if it's in the expected but not in result, everything fails even if the test fully matches. They think that's unfair.\n\nI'm in plan mode — read-only. I need to analyze and plan, not edit.\n\nLet me address:\n\n1. §4.6 assessment of check.cpp. Let me re-examine the current messages:\n- L37: \"3 arguments expected (--flag <file_result> <file_expected>). Received {} arguments\" — good: what's expected, what received.\n- L44: \"Incorrect flag {}. Expected --contains or --equal\" — content good; \"Incorrect\" is judgment; \"Unknown\" more factual. Minor.\n- L52/59: \"Failed to open {}\" — what + target, but no cause. §4.6 asks for system error text. Also consistency with run_case.cpp which includes the OS error. Since these are test tools, is it needed? The course says applies to every diagnostic message from the first task. So arguably should include reason. But: `file_to_bytes_string` returns `std::unexpected(1)` — no error carried. To include reason, need to capture errno/error_code. That's a real improvement but touches the return type. Hmm. Also the tool messages are English now (for cross-platform). §4.6 is about content, not language.\n- L69: \"Output file {} doesnt contain all data from {}\" — content good; fix \"doesnt\". Semantics: \"doesn't contain all data from expected file\" — good.\n- L78: \"Files {} {} are not equal even after removing unnecessary CR-s and LF-s\" — content good; wording awkward.\n\nAlso, §4.6 requires no \"Ошибка:\"/\"Error:\" prefixes — none present. Neutral tone — good. No accusations — good.\n\nIs it \"предметным\" enough? Mostly yes. The main gap: failure reason for open failures, and small wording nits. Also consistency: use \"Cannot\" (like