ncidental.\n\nNormalizing before `contains`:\n- Apply the same CR removal / trailing-LF trimming to both `result` and `expected`, then `find`. This makes it robust to line-ending and trailing-newline differences.\n- Tradeoff: loses the ability to assert exact line endings via `--contains`. But `--equal` is the strict comparison tool.\n\nBut careful: there's an important subtlety. `--contains` is used for error messages. Example expected file: `N должно быть целым числом. Получено: abc` (no trailing newline in expect, but result has `\\r\\n`). Currently `find` works because expected has no trailing newline and is a substring. But if expected had a trailing newline and result had `\\r\\n`, `find(\"...\\n\")` in `\"...\\r\\n\"` would fail because between `.` and `\\n` there's `\\r`. Actually if expected = \"...abc\\n\" and result = \"...abc\\r\\n\", find fails. So CRLF is the real culprit for contains, not just trailing newline.\n\nThe user says \"нормализовывать по CRLF в обоих ветках\" — i.e., apply rm_crlf to both in contains too. That removes `\\r` and trailing `\\n`. That makes contains robust to CRLF and trailing blank lines. Seems reasonable.\n\nBut wait: their `expect` files were converted to LF, and Windows programs write CRLF (stdout text mode) — the journal said \"при тестировании на нормальных данных выход и эталон сравнивается полностью. при ошибочных случаях строка/строки эталона ищутся в выходном файле\". And they converted expect/input to LF to avoid CRLF issues. And `.out`/`.err` files from programs contain CRLF (text mode) — we saw `0D 0A` in the err file. So indeed result files have CRLF, expect files have LF. For `--contains`, expected (LF) inside result (CRLF): if the expected fragment doesn't include a line ending, find works. If it does (multi-line expected), the LF won't match CRLF → fails. So normalizing CR makes multi-line contains work.\n\nSo the user's proposal has real merit: normalize CR in both branches. But there's a subtlety about trailing `\\n` trimming: `rm_crlf_symbols` also pops trailing `\\n`s. For `contains`, popping trailing newlines from both changes semantics slightly: an expected fragment \"...\\n\" becomes \"...\" → matches more. That's the \"fairness\" the user wants.\n\nHowever, is normalizing in the contains branch a good idea, or could it mask real bugs? Consider a test asserting that stderr ends with a newline (a legit requirement — diagnostics should end with `\\n`). With normalization, you can't check that via contains. But you could via `--equal`. Since `--equal` also normalizes trailing LFs (rm_crlf_symbols), you can't check trailing newline in either branch! Hmm — actually `rm_crlf_symbols` in the equal branch already trims trailing newlines, so exact line-ending checks aren't possible anywhere. So adding it to contains doesn't reduce capability that exists elsewhere.\n\nWait, does `rm_crlf_symbols` erase ALL `\\r` (not just before `\\n`)? `std::erase(s, '\\r')` removes every CR. Then pops trailing `\\n`s. So it's a normalization: CRLF→LF, strip trailing newlines. Fine.\n\nProposal: factor a single helper `normalize(std::string&)` = remove CR + strip trailing LF, and apply in both branches. That's DRY (removes the duplication risk; §4.9 п.9 \"повторила дважды — выноси\"). Actually the rm_crlf is already a helper and would be called in both branches — so it's fine.\n\nBut there's a design question: should the helper be applied *before* the branch, once to both strings? That's cleaner: normalize result and expected once at the top of both branches, then branch. But be careful: normalizing mutates both for both flags; then `contains` uses find on normalized, `equal` compares normalized. That unifies behavior. Actually simplest: after reading both, call `rm_crlf_symbols(result); rm_crlf_symbols(expected);` unconditionally, then branch. Then both flags operate on normalized text. This is DRY and consistent.\n\nDownside: if someone wants a strict byte comparison (including CRLF/trailing newline), neither flag offers it. Is that acceptable? The project's policy (journal) already leaned to tolerating CR/LF. The design has `--equal` as \"equality after CR/LF normalization\". So yes.\n\nHmm, but wait: is there a need for a strict mode? The task's tests compare stdout for \"normal data\" — full equality expected after normalization. Given cross-platform (LF expect, CRLF output on Windows), normalization is necessary. The journal explicitly says they decided this. So unify.\n\nBut careful about one thing: the user says \"нормализовывать по CRLF в обоих ветках check\". I should raise the tradeoff: normalizing in contains also removes the ability to distinguish, but since equal already normalizes, no loss. Also mention: this is a *policy* decision that should be documented (in check.cpp comment or README): both modes compare after normalizing line endings (CR removed, trailing LFs stripped), so exact line endings are not asserted.\n\nAnother subtlety: `rm_crlf_symbols` strips trailing `\\n` but leaves internal blank lines. For contains, trailing strip could make an expected fragment that's whitespace-only become empty → empty string is a substring of everything → test always passes. Edge case: if expected file is only newlines, after normalization it's empty → `find(\"\")` returns 0 → always pass. That's a potential false-positive. Probably not a real case, but worth a guard (e.g., expected non-empty). Minor.\n\nAlso: normalization for contains with internal CRLF vs LF: after erasing CR, both become LF, so multi-line fragments match. Good.\n\nAnother consideration: does the normalization belong to *check* (the test tool) or should the *generator of expect files* be consistent? They already normalized expect/data to LF. But the program output on Windows is CRLF. So the tool must normalize. Alternatively, run programs so they output LF (binary stdout)? No — CRT text mode adds CR on Windows. Could set stdout to binary mode (`_setmode(_O_BINARY)`) so output is LF on both platforms — that's another, arguably cleaner approach (the program's output would be identical across platforms). Hmm! That's a real design alternative: make the programs write LF (binary mode) so expect (LF) matches exactly and no normalization needed. But that changes test semantics and requires changes in each task program (or a shared init). Also ASan/CRT text mode. That's a bigger change and touches task programs. The user's current path (normalize in check) is simpler and they already decided. I should mention the alternative briefly but not derail.\n\nActually, given AGENTS §4.9 п.4 (cross-platform), making output LF-consistent is attractive. But it's a bigger, cross-cutting decision; mention as an option, recommend keeping normalization for now (it also absorbs the \"trailing newline\" brittleness they care about).\n\nNow, note also: the user's motivation \"если он есть в эталоне а в result нету\" — trailing newline. But normalizing trailing newlines away means a test can't catch a missing final newline. For stderr diagnostics, a missing final newline is a minor style issue; tolerating it is reasonable.\n\nLet me also verify: does `rm_crlf_symbols` handle `\\r` at end without `\\n`? It erases all `\\r` anyway. Fine.\n\nPlan for Q2:\n- Unify normalization: normalize both `result` and `expected` once (before branching), so `--contains` and `--equal` behave consistently.\n- Optionally guard: if `expected` becomes empty and flag is contains, that's suspicious — but CMake passes non-empty expect files. Could leave.\n- Update the comment to document the comparison policy.\n- Add/adjust tests? There's no unit test for check itself, but the ctest suite exercises it. Could add a case where expect has LF and result CRLF (already the norm). Hmm, currently tests pass because err files' contains fragments are single-line. A multi-line contains case would validate the change. Optional.\n\nNow, does Q2 affect `check.cpp` structure much? Minimal:\nOption A (unify): after reading result/expected, call rm_crlf_symbols on both, remove the calls from the equal branch.\nThat's the minimal change and DRY.\n\nLet me double check: in equal branch currently rm_crlf called on both then compare. Moving it up = same behavior for equal, new behavior for contains (normalized). Good.\n\nOne concern: for `--contains` on binary-ish data, removing CR could alter content. But these are text files. Fine.\n\nNow Q1 answer: recommendation to make more substantive:\n- open-failure reason (needs file_to_bytes_string to carry the error). Options: return `std::expected<std::string, std::error_code>` or `int` with errno captured. Since fstream doesn't expose errno cleanly, could use `errno` after failed open (std::fstream sets errno? Implementation-defined but typically yes via fopen). Alternatively use `std::ifstream` and `std::error_code(errno, generic_category())`. Hmm. Or use `std::filesystem`? Overkill.\n  Simpler: keep int code but print `errno` message. In `file_to_bytes_string`, on failure capture `errno` and return it; then print context + error message. But `failure` may not set errno reliably. Let me flag as \"if you want the OS reason, capture errno right after the failed open; verify it's set\".\n  Actually, is it worth it for check? §4.6 applies, but the practical value: when a test fails because the expected file is missing (wrong path), the reason \"no such file\" is obvious from the path. It's marginal. I'd present as optional.\n- wording nits: \"Incorrect\"→\"Unknown\"; \"doesnt\"→\"does not\"; \"Failed to open\"→\"Cannot open\"; \"CR-s and LF-s\"→\"CR/LF\".\n- Consider including *where* equal-files differ (first differing offset / line) — this is the most useful addition for a test tool. But it adds code; and the files are in results/ for inspection. Optional.\n\nSo answer Q1: it's already largely compliant; the substantive improvements are optional. Recommend at least the wording nits and, if desired, the diff location.\n\nNow, since plan mode, I must produce a plan and ask. Let me also consider: the user said \"1. мне стоит сделать check.cpp более предметным или он уже нормален по 4.6?\" — direct question needing an assessment. And 2 is a design proposal I should validate/critique.\n\nAnti-sycophancy: I should critically evaluate their CRLF idea rather than just agree. Points:\n- Agree it's reasonable and consistent.\n- But note: normalizing in contains reduces strictness; make sure that's intended and documented.\n- Note the empty-expected edge case.\n- Note alternative (binary stdout / LF everywhere) exists but is bigger.\n- Also: currently there's a real asymmetry: equal normalizes, contains doesn't — that's inconsistent; unifying is good.\n\nAlso, I should double check: is the current `--equal` already trimming trailing `\\n`? Yes. So the \"trailing newline\" concern applies to equal too (it's already tolerant). So contains is the only strict one. Unifying removes the asymmetry. Good.\n\nLet me also verify what the actual current expect/err files look like re trailing newline, to ground the discussion. Earlier: err file ended `0D 0A` (CRLF). expect `t1_abc` ended `... 61 62 64` (no newline). So for contains, expected has no trailing newline → find works. If they had written expected with a trailing newline, it'd break. So the concern is real for future multi-line/trailing-newline expects.\n\nNow, let me also think: should normalization happen for `--equal` on trailing newlines? They already do. So consistent.\n\nLet me formulate the plan:\n\nQ1 plan (optional refinements to check.cpp):\n- Messages: \n  - L44 `Incorrect flag` → `Unknown flag`\n  - L52/59 `Failed to open {}` → `Cannot open {}: {}` with reason (optional; requires carrying errno)\n  - L69 `doesnt` → `does not`\n  - L78 `CR-s and LF-s` → `CR/LF`\n- Optional: for `--equal` mismatch, report first differing position (byte offset / line) — a concrete \"какое значение получено\". \n- Evaluate: are these needed? Recommend yes for the wording; reason/diff optional.\n\nQ2 plan:\n- Move normalization before the branch: normalize both `result` and `expected` once; then `--contains` does find, `--equal` compares.\n- Document the policy in a comment (both modes ignore CR and trailing LFs).\n- Guard: if expected normalizes to empty, treat specially? (avoid vacuous pass) — optional.\n- Test: add a multi-line contains case (expect LF vs result CRLF) to lock the behavior; or a case with trailing newline in expect.\n- Consider naming: `normalize_line_endings`.\n\nAlso mention: this makes check's behavior consistent with the decision recorded in the journal (CR/LF tolerance).\n\nAlso, potential issue: `rm_crlf_symbols` currently only strips trailing `\\n`, not trailing `\\r` alone — but it erases all `\\r` first, so a trailing `\\r` becomes nothing then trailing `\\n` stripped. Fine.\n\nLet me also think about whether normalizing contains could hide a genuine newline-position error: e.g., expected has words on separate lines but result has them on one line. After removing CR, no change to LF; trailing LF stripped. Multi-line vs single-line still differ → still fails. Good, normalization only removes CR and trailing LFs, preserving internal LF structure. So it's a targeted tolerance.\n\nWait, but the user said \"нормализовывать по CRLF\" — maybe they mean convert CRLF→LF (keep newlines), not strip trailing. The current helper strips trailing too. For contains, stripping trailing is what handles \"эталон с \\n, result без\". So they'd use the same helper (which strips trailing). Good — I'll clarify what the helper does and that reusing it gives both behaviors.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: if we strip trailing newlines in contains, and expected is a multi-line fragment ending with `\\n`, stripping it means it can match result where the fragment is followed by more text. That's fine for contains.\n\nEdge: expected = \"a\\nb\\n\" → normalized \"a\\nb\"; result = \"a\\nb\\nc\" → contains true. Reasonable.\n\nNow let me also flag a potential downside the user may not have considered: by normalizing trailing newlines in `--contains`, you can no longer detect extra trailing blank lines — but that's already true for `--equal`. So no loss.\n\nOK.\n\nLet me also make sure about §4.6 applicability to `check`: These messages are for the human reading test output; they don't have differentiated exit codes. §4.6 applies to all diagnostic messages. Their content is mostly fine. I'll give a verdict: \"по §4.6 — уже почти нормально; формально содержательно, нет штампов; можно подтянуть X,Y,Z\".\n\nAlso mention: the grep self-check: run `grep -nE \"Ошибка|Error|Failed|...\"` — now check.cpp has no Russian and \"Failed\" appears twice (L52/59) → review them. Since \"Failed to open X\" carries content, it passes, but consider \"Cannot open X: reason\".\n\nLet me now write the answer as a plan with two parts and questions. Keep concise but complete.\n\nI must not edit. End with asking for decisions:\n- Q1: apply wording nits only, or also add reason/diff?\n- Q2: confirm unify normalization; decide on empty-expected guard; add tests?\n- Then I can prepare the exact edits when out of plan mode.\n\nLet me write.\n\nActually, let me double-check the exact current check.cpp lines to reference correctly (I have them). L37, 44, 52, 59, 69, 78. Good.\n\nLet me write the response.",
