          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks: how problematic is it to add errno output and the location where files differ (first differing byte/line) in the `--equal` branch. They note this is the most valuable for a test tool, \"what value was received\" per §4.6, but adds code and duplicates what's visible in results/*.\n\nI need to assess complexity concretely, with a plan. Let me analyze the current check.cpp structure.\n\nCurrent:\n- `file_to_bytes_string(const char* path) -> std::expected<std::string, int>`: opens `std::fstream` binary, on failure returns `std::unexpected(1)`. No errno captured.\n- `rm_crlf_symbols`.\n- main: argc check, flag check, read r1, read r2, branch contains/equal.\n\nPart A: add errno to open failures.\n- Problem: `std::fstream::open` (i.e., `basic_filebuf::open`) — does it set `errno`? The underlying `fopen`/`_Fsopen` sets errno, but the standard doesn't guarantee fstream propagates errno. In practice MSVC's fstream uses `_Fiopen` which uses `_fsopen` and sets errno. But relying on it is implementation-defined. Also failures can be \"file not found\", \"permission denied\", \"is a directory\".\n- Cleaner: capture `std::error_code` via `errno` right after `is_open()` check, but errno may be stale. Alternatively use `std::filesystem`? Not for reading bytes.\n- Standard-ish approach: use `std::ifstream` and check; for the reason, you can't portably get it. Options:\n  1. Use C `std::fopen` + `errno`, then read via `fread` into string. Portable and gives errno. But changes the reading style (they currently use istreambuf_iterator, which is idiomatic C++). Could keep istreambuf_iterator on an `std::ifstream` — but then no errno.\n  2. Keep fstream; after `!is_open()`, read `errno` immediately. Documented as \"implementation sets errno in practice; verify\". On MSVC it works; on libstdc++ too usually. Mark as [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ].\n  3. Use `std::optional`/error_code and, on Windows, capture `GetLastError`? Not portable.\n- Complexity: low. Change return