 sketch (as plan, not apply) and estimate. Let me write the sketch:\n\n```cpp\n// возвращает позицию первого расхождения в нормализованных строках\nstruct diff_position { std::size_t offset; std::size_t line; };\n\ndiff_position first_difference(std::string_view a, std::string_view b) {\n    auto [ia, ib] = std::mismatch(a.begin(), a.end(), b.begin(), b.end());\n    std::size_t offset = static_cast<std::size_t>(ia - a.begin());\n    std::size_t line = 1 + static_cast<std::size_t>(std::count(a.begin(), ia, '\\n'));\n    return {offset, line};\n}\n```\nThen message: for each mismatch, print line and offset, plus maybe the differing byte.\n\nFor errno: change `file_to_bytes_string` to return `std::expected<std::string, int>` where int = errno (or `std::error_code`). On failure: `return std::unexpected(errno);` and print `std::error_code(r.error(), std::generic_category()).message()`.\n\nHmm, but the existing failure returns `1` which is also used as the process exit code (r1.error() returned from main → exit 1). If errno = 2 (ENOENT) then main would return 2, changing exit code! Currently main does `return r1.error();` → returns 1 always for open failure. If we make error = errno, exit code becomes errno — violating their decision \"any error = 1\" for service programs. So we must decouple: keep exit code 1, but carry the errno for the message. So the expected should carry a struct {code, errno} or main should `return 1;` while printing the reason.\n\nGood catch — worth noting in the plan. So either:\n- `std::expected<std::string, std::error_code>`; main prints message and returns 1 (not ec.value()). That preserves exit code 1.\n- Or keep int as errno and `return 1;` explicitly.\n\nThat's a subtle but important design point. Let me include it.\n\nAlso: `std::error_code(errno, std::generic_category())` — for Windows file errors, `generic_category` maps errno to messages (strerror). Fine. Could use `system_category` but generic is standard for errno.\n\nNow, let me also reconsider whether errno is worth it given complexity. My honest assessment:\n- errno: low complexity, but requires touching the return type and being careful about the exit code; plus the portability caveat. Value: moderate (reason for missing file is often obvious).\n- diff location: low-moderate complexity (~20 lines with std::mismatch), high value for a test tool. No portability issues.\nSo I'd recommend the diff location more than errno. But both are fine.\n\nDuplication concern: printing line number/offset is a *pointer* to results/*, not a full duplicate. Printing the whole differing line would duplicate. Suggest printing offset+line+byte values, optionally a short escaped snippet. Keep it terse.\n\nAlso note: the normalization means offsets are in normalized text; if we report line numbers, they should match the raw file's lines as long as CR removal doesn't change line count (it doesn't; it removes CR only) and trailing-LF strip doesn't affect earlier lines. So line numbers correspond to the file's lines. Byte offsets differ from the raw file (CR removed). So report line (safe) and maybe offset within the line, or just line + the differing bytes. Good.\n\nHmm, actually to make it maximally useful without duplication: \"first difference at line 3\" and maybe show the two lines? Showing the line duplicates results/*. I'd show the line number and the differing characters. Let me present options.\n\nNow, plan mode: present the assessment + plan + ask. No edits.\n\nLet me also consider `std::mismatch` with two ranges: `std::mismatch(first1, last1, first2, last2)` (C++14 4-iterator overload). Use that to know which ended. Good.\n\nLet me estimate lines:\n- diff helper: ~10 lines.\n- errno change: ~5 lines changed.\nTotal ~15-20 lines. \"Проблематичность\" — низкая.\n\nLet me write the answer concisely with:\n1. Difficulty verdict.\n2. errno: what changes, the exit-code gotcha, the errno portability caveat (and mitigation).\n3. diff: sketch with std::mismatch, note about normalized positions/lines.\n4. Recommendation + options.\n5. Updated plan and questions.\n\nLet me be careful to present code as a proposal (plan), not apply.\n\nLet me write.",
