          "text": "The user asks directly: do I recommend making these additions to check, or is it already self-sufficient now?\n\nI should give a clear recommendation (anti-sycophancy: be direct, not just \"up to you\"). Weigh:\n- check.cpp currently works and is §4.6-compliant in content.\n- It's a service/test tool, not a deliverable task. §4.9 п.2 says helper logic should be minimized; §4.2 warns against over-engineering.\n- The stage: they still have pending work (the `<format.h>`/`<ranges.h>` fix, building, tests). check is a means, not the goal.\n- The CRLF normalization is the one change with real functional value (correctness of test outcomes), not just diagnostics. That one I'd recommend: it removes a cross-platform brittleness and an asymmetry.\n- The diff-location and errno are diagnostics quality-of-life. For a test tool used only by them, benefit is moderate; cost is small. But they can also just open results/*. And there's a risk of scope creep.\n\nSo my honest recommendation:\n- Must/strongly yes: CRLF normalization do-before-branch (functional correctness/consistency).\n- Nice-to-have, low priority: messages cosmetics (trivial, do when touching the file).\n- Optional/defer: diff location — actually valuable when debugging, but defer until it's needed (YAGNI). errno — defer.\n- The user's own rule (§4.2 trivalent check: \"зачем этот фрагмент существует — решает реальную проблему?\") argues against adding diagnostics that aren't yet needed.\n\nHmm, but earlier I said diff location is \"high value\". Now the user asks whether to do it. Let me be consistent but decisive: I recommend doing the CRLF normalization (real function), the small wording nits (free, since touching the file), and deferring diff/errno until actually needed. Rationale: YAGNI, check is a means, and the data is in results/* for now.\n\nActually, is the diff genuinely not needed? When a test fails, you have the .out/.err files and can open them. A line number saves time. But is it worth code now? The user is learning; adding a well-scoped diagnostic is also a learning exercise, but priority should be the pending task (get tests green, fix headers).\n\nLet me also note: there's a risk with the CRLF change — it changes test semantics; must confirm with tests. And it needs to be done together with the check.cpp header/formatting.\n\nLet me give a crisp verdict:\n\nVerdict: check самодостаточен функционально. Обязательна к внесению только нормализация CR/LF (это не косметика, а устранение асимметрии/ложных падений). Косметика сообщений — если уже правишь файл (почти бесплатно). Диагностику (diff/errno) — отложить до реальной потребности (YAGNI, §4.2/триступенчатая проверка), потому что данные и так в results/*.\n\nThen answer the direct question: \"ты советуешь делать эти дополнения?\" → no, not now; only normalization + minor wording.\n\nAlso mention: prioritize the outstanding correctness items: `<format.h>`/`<ranges.h>` fix, build+ctest green, journal entry, commit, dialog export.\n\nLet me keep it concise. Plan mode: no edits. Present recommendation; ask if they agree, and then the plan for the minimal set.\n\nLet me structure:\n1. Прямой ответ: самодостаточен; дополнения — не обязательны.\n2. Что рекомендую сделать (минимум): normalization (functional) + wording nits (free).\n3. Что отложить: diff location, errno — с обоснованием (YAGNI, real problem?, data in results/*).\n4. Что важнее сейчас: header fix, build/test green, journal, commit/export.\n5. Ask: согласна на минимальный набор?\n\nAlso note: if they do the diff later, the plan is ready.\n\nLet me write it.",
