          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The spec confirms:\n- Section names are filepath globs.\n- Special chars: `*`, `**`, `?`, `[seq]`, `[!seq]`, `{s1,s2,s3}` (any of the strings separated by commas, can be nested), `{num1..num2}`.\n- **There is NO comma-separated list outside braces.** So `[*.md, CMakeLists.txt, *.cmake]` is a *single glob* where `,` and spaces are literal characters (spaces allowed inside brackets per file format). It would only match a file literally named with \", \" — essentially never. So NOT equivalent.\n\nAlso: braces `{s1,s2,s3}` — \"any of the strings given (separated by commas, can be nested)\". Note the strings are taken as-is; the spec doesn't mention trimming whitespace inside braces. But the file format says \"Remove all leading and trailing whitespace\" only for the whole line, and section header \"May contain any characters between the square brackets (e.g., `[` and `]` and even spaces and tabs are allowed).\" So spaces inside are significant.\n\nNow, in the user's second form `[{*.md, *.cmake, CMakeLists.txt}]`, the alternatives after brace expansion are literally `*.md`, ` *.cmake`, ` CMakeLists.txt` — with a leading space for the 2nd and 3rd. Would that break? The spec says `{s1,s2,s3}` = any of the strings separated by commas. It doesn't say trim. So strictly, ` *.cmake` includes a leading space and would try to match filenames starting with a space — would not match `format.cmake`.\n\nHmm, but do cores trim inside braces in practice? The core-tests might define behavior. Let me think: editorconfig-core-py's `fnmatch`/brace expansion: In `editorconfig` Python core, `_fnmatch` uses `re` translation; brace expansion implemented via `_BraceExpander`? It splits on commas without trimming, I believe. Actually, there's a known subtlety: `{a, b}` might be treated with the space. Let me check the core-tests or source.\n\nActually, let me check the editorconfig-core-test repository for a test about spaces in braces. Hmm, this is getting deep. But it's directly the user's question: are the two forms equivalent? And also, is spacing inside braces safe?\n\nLet me verify with the reference core implementation. The Python core: https://github.com/editorconfig/editorconfig-core-py. Its `editorconfig/fnmatch.py` implements the translator. Let me recall/check: In `fnmatch.py`, there's handling of `{...}` that expands to `(?:...|...)`; the alternatives are the literal substrings between commas. I don't recall trimming.\n\nActually, there's a known EditorConfig behavior: whitespace after commas IS significant and is NOT trimmed, per implementation. Some linters warn about `[*.{js, jsx}]` with spaces because it would match `*.js` or ` *.jsx` (with leading space). Hmm, actually wait — many people write spaces and claim it works. Let me check the editorconfig-core-py source to be sure.\n\nLet me fetch the fnmatch source from the Python core.\n\nActually, more authoritative: the core-tests. But let me fetch editorconfig-core-py's `fnmatch.py` and inspect brace expansion.\n\nLet me webfetch https://raw.githubusercontent.com/editorconfig/editorconfig-core-py/master/editorconfig/fnmatch.py\n\nLet me do that.\n\nThen I can answer definitively:\n1. Not equivalent — bare commas aren't a thing; the brace form is the correct one.\n2. Spaces inside braces may be literal → safest to write without spaces: `[{*.md,*.cmake,CMakeLists.txt}]`.\n\nLet me fetch to confirm the whitespace behavior.\n\nActually, let me also consider: the current `.editorconfig` that the user says they fixed — I should re-read it to see exactly what they wrote, so I can tell them if it's correct. The user said \".editorconfig сделала utf-8 и добавила *.cmake в utf-8\". Let me read the file to see the exact section syntax. That's important to answer their question in context.\n\nLet me read `.editorconfig` and fetch fnmatch.py in parallel.\n\nLet me do it.",
