          "text": "The user asks: \"то есть если генерализовать мысль: кодировка важна когда начинаются национальные алфавиты. если англ - то не так важно?\"\n\nThey want a generalized statement. I should confirm and refine precisely, with nuance.\n\nAnswer: Roughly yes, but let me sharpen it:\n\n- If content is pure ASCII (English letters, digits, punctuation, whitespace), all common encodings agree byte-for-byte → encoding is irrelevant. ASCII is a subset of CP1251, CP866, UTF-8, latin1, etc.\n- Encoding becomes relevant as soon as there are non-ASCII characters (national alphabets: Cyrillic, etc.).\n- BUT: \"англ\" isn't quite the right criterion. The criterion is **ASCII vs non-ASCII**, not \"English vs national\". Examples where \"English-looking\" content still has non-ASCII:\n  - Typographic punctuation: curly quotes \" \" ' ', em-dash —, ellipsis …, non-breaking space. These are non-ASCII (in UTF-8: 3 bytes; CP1251 has some like — … but not curly quotes necessarily). English text often contains these.\n  - Trademark ©, ®, degree °.\n  - So \"English\" text with smart quotes is non-ASCII.\n- Also: the reverse — a national alphabet CAN be represented in ASCII via transliteration, but that's not encoding.\n\nSo the precise generalization: **важна не «англ vs национальный», а «ASCII vs не-ASCII»**. Pure ASCII — кодировка не важна; любой не-ASCII символ — важна (выбор кодировки, BOM, согласованность между записью и чтением).\n\nAdditional nuance: \"не так важно\" — depends on context:\n- If a program treats bytes as opaque (no decoding), encoding doesn't matter even for non-ASCII — e.g., binary copy, or `--contains` byte comparison we discussed. Encoding only matters when something *interprets* bytes as text (renders, compares case-insensitively, uppercases, parses).\n- Even with non-ASCII, if writer and reader agree, it works. Problems arise from *mismatch* (writer CP1251, reader UTF-8) or from tools that assume UTF-8.\n\nAlso: line endings (CRLF/LF) and BOM are